## Method

Graded on the wire with **`tools/arpleg.py --tcp`**, the tool run 1 extended for this case. A
management filter has three possible answers and only the wire separates them: `SA` = open,
`R`/`RA` = the service refused, and **no reply row at all** = the frame was silently dropped by
the filter. `show access-list`, `show access-list counters` and `show ssh server` can
distinguish none of them.

The filter is the **named** hardware form, because run 1 proved the numbered form silently
collapses to one rule:
```
awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
```
That is TPS 6.1.1's statement in two lines: the **user-specified host** `10.38.22.33` reaches
management SSH, every other host does not.

Every leg carries two controls:
- an **ICMP echo to the same management address**, which must be answered -- so "the filter
  dropped it" is never confused with "the interface is down";
- on the eth3 leg, **TCP/23**, a service that is off, which must answer `RA` -- so a *refused*
  frame and a *filtered* frame are visibly different in the same capture.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag <tag> --out /tmp/ntp1021/T33386 --iface eth4 \
     --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 --icmp 10.38.22.66 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f \
     --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
sudo arpleg.py --tag <tag> --out /tmp/ntp1021/T33386 --iface eth3 \
     --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:23 --icmp 10.38.22.34 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e \
     --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
```

## The limb

### LEG A -- baseline, no filter (tag `t386-base-eth4`)

```
  cap t386-base-eth4-eth4.pcap: 24 frame(s)
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.66:22    -> 10.38.22.65:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   R
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]
```
**4 of 4 `SA`** -- the non-specified host reaches management SSH with no filter in place. The
instrument and the leg are proved before any silence is read.

### LEG B -- filter applied (tags `t386-acl-eth4`, `t386-acl-eth3`)

```
awplus#show access-list
Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
    4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
    8 deny tcp any any eq 22
```
```
  cap t386-acl-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)                <- the DENIED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]

  cap t386-acl-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)                <- the PERMITTED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]
```
Denied host: **4 SYNs, no reply of any kind**, ICMP 4 of 4. Permitted host: **4 `SA`**, with
`:23` answering `RA`. Both halves of the filter are working before the reboot.

### LEG C -- the limb: after a cold boot, nothing typed

```
awplus#write
Building configuration...
[OK]
awplus#show startup-config | include access-list|access-group|permit|deny
access-list hardware ckmgmt
 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
 deny tcp any any eq 22
 access-group ckmgmt
 access-group ckmgmt

tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
17:46:05 >>> reload   (04:46:05 UTC)
reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
17:47:05 login seen after 60 s
```

🔬 **The legs were settled before they were read**, applying T33380 run 2's lesson from 20
minutes earlier. The first `ping` on **eth3 after the boot returned 0 of 4, 100% packet loss**;
a second, seconds later, returned **4 of 4** and the box's neighbour table read
`10.38.22.34 dev eth3 lladdr e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 REACHABLE`. eth4 was 4 of 4 immediately. Had the
leg been taken at the first attempt, eth3's "no TCP reply rows" would have read as **the filter
blocking the permitted host** -- the exact opposite of the truth.

The real legs, 36 s after the login prompt, **nothing typed into the DUT**:
```
  cap t386-afterboot-eth4-eth4.pcap: 14 frame(s)          <- the DENIED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.65 tell 10.38.22.66
    x1   ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.65 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f

  cap t386-afterboot-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)          <- the PERMITTED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]
```

**Both halves survived.** The deny still drops the non-specified host silently in hardware while
its ICMP is answered 4 of 4, and the permit still lets the specified host complete a handshake,
with the `RA` control still telling a refused service from a filtered frame.

Only then was the console opened:
```
awplus#show access-list
Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
    4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
    8 deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus#show running-config | include access-group
access-group ckmgmt
 access-group ckmgmt
awplus#show access-list counters
Hardware ACL Packet Counters
Name: ckmgmt
	Rule: permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
	Packet Hits: 8
	Rule: deny tcp any any eq 22
	Packet Hits: 4
```
🔬 **The counters were reset by the reboot and account for the post-boot legs exactly**:
`deny = 4` is eth4's four SYNs; `permit = 8` is eth3's four SYNs plus its four RSTs, both
matching `tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22`. The device's own counter and the wire agree with no
residue from before the reload -- which also makes the counters a usable second witness here,
unlike `show ssh server` in T33380.

### LEG D -- removal (tag `t386-removed-eth4`)

```
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt          [port1.0.10]
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt          [port1.0.48]
awplus(config)#no access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus#show access-list
   (nothing)
```
```
  cap t386-removed-eth4-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.66:22    -> 10.38.22.65:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   R
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 ... [ours] ; x4 ICMP t0/c0 ... [ours]
```
The denied host is served again. **The arc on the wire: `SA` -> dropped -> (cold boot) dropped
-> `SA`.**

